<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/expert/multilingual-cognition">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Multilingual cognition asks <strong>what happens to a mind that runs multiple languages</strong>
    — how they co-activate, interfere, enrich and reshape thought. It is where language learning
    meets cognitive science, and where several famous claims (the bilingual advantage, Sapir–Whorf)
    have been tested hard.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="co-activation, the advantage debate, Whorf, and the bilingual experience" widget="equationStep" config={multilingCfg} />

  <h2>The practitioner's takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Interference is normal, not failure:</strong> reaching for the wrong language word, mixing in casual speech — normal operation of a co-activated system, not a defect to eliminate.</li>
    <li><strong>Each language stays a separate personality:</strong> multilinguals report shifting emotional register and even values across languages — real, measurable (framing effects differ by language), and part of why the effort is worth it.</li>
    <li><strong>L2 changes L1:</strong> learning a language fine-tunes your native one — vocabulary for concepts you lacked, awareness of your own grammar. The bilingual\'s first language is also a learner\'s language.</li>
    <li><strong>Third languages come easier:</strong> L3 acquisition benefits from L2 strategies (metalinguistic awareness, learning-how-to-learn) — the first foreign language is the hardest; the infrastructure compounds (the languages ladder\'s whole arc, paid forward).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Grosjean, Life with Two Languages; Kroll & Bialystok, Trends in Cognitive Sciences (2013); Kay & Kempton (1984) on Whorf effects.</p>
</TopicPage>
